From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Philippe Simons <simons.philippe@gmail.com>
Date: Mon, 7 Sep 2026 12:30:00 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] clk: qcom: dispcc-sm8550 mdp RCG ops + Lucid Ole PLL guard

TWO HUNKS, ONE PATCH, DELIBERATELY. They are a pair: the second is fatal without
the first, in a way nothing reports. Splitting them into two files invites a bump
to drop one, so they cannot be separated here.

Both are backports from "SM8450 QoL changes (dispcc)" (lore.kernel.org, Esteban
Urrutia, Reviewed-by Konrad Dybcio), neither in the pinned 7.2.3.

1. clk-alpha-pll.c -- "Check Lucid Ole PLL status before configuring"
   (Fixes: 3132a9a11e57). Skip clk_lucid_ole_pll_configure() when the PLL is
   already enabled, because "there are RCGs that will lock up if we disable the
   PLL below". This is an OMISSION being corrected, not new behaviour: 7.2.3
   already carries the identical guard in three siblings in the same file --
   Trion (:1723), Lucid 5LPE (:1945), Lucid Evo (:2341) -- and Lucid Ole is the
   only one without it.

   It reaches us because our boards hand over with the display already lit (the
   cont_splash carve-out), so disp_cc_pll0/pll1 are RUNNING when dispcc-sm8550.c
   probes and calls clk_lucid_ole_pll_configure() on both unconditionally
   (:1797-1798). That is what produced, on every boot:

     [    0.697] disp_cc_mdss_mdp_clk_src: rcg didn't update its configuration.
                 update_config <- clk_rcg2_shared_init, CPU#7 swapper/0/1

   The warning was a SYMPTOM of the PLL being reprogrammed under a live RCG.

2. dispcc-sm8550.c -- "Fix disp_cc_mdss_mdp_clk_src ops", retargeted from
   dispcc-sm8450 (same node, same shape). clk_rcg2_shared_no_init_park_ops leaves
   the frequency alone at registration instead of parking the RCG, which upstream
   wants because the park shows as a flicker on a bootloader-lit panel -- ours.

   This also drops a LOCAL GUESS that lived here: CLK_OPS_PARENT_ENABLE, whose
   commit message claimed it fixed the warning above. It never did -- the flag
   covers .set_rate and .set_parent while the failing update comes from .init --
   and the warning survived it for as long as it was in the tree. Removing it
   changes nothing observable. The node is now byte-for-byte the upstream shape,
   matching dispcc-x1e80100.

COVERS BOTH SHIPPING SoCs: dispcc-sm8550.c serves SM8650 too, via
`{ .compatible = "qcom,sm8650-dispcc" }` in its match table.

WHY THEY CANNOT BE SPLIT, measured on an AYANEO Pocket ACE:

  hunk 2 alone, no flag      -> BLACK PANEL   (build 20260907T094603Z)
  hunk 2 alone, with flag    -> BLACK PANEL   (build 20260907T100424Z)
  hunk 1 alone               -> works, warning 0 (build 20260907T120035Z)
  both (this)                -> works, warning 0 (build 20260907T122602Z)

Without the .init park the RCG stays sourced from whatever the bootloader left
running, and without hunk 1 probe then reprograms exactly that PLL underneath it
-- the lock-up hunk 1 exists to prevent. And the failure is INVISIBLE to every
remote probe: the black builds reported no warning, no underrun, no vblank
timeout, backlight on, connector connected, gamescope presenting and flips
completing. Only a human looking at the panel could tell them from a good build.

REACHES MORE THAN THE DISPLAY, untested: gpucc-sm8550.c and gpucc-sm8650.c call
clk_lucid_ole_pll_configure() too, so a bootloader-enabled GPU PLL is no longer
reconfigured at probe either. Suspect hunk 1 first for any GPU-side surprise.

The sm8450 halves are upstream; these sm8550 retargets are not, and should be
sent.

Signed-off-by: Esteban Urrutia <esteuwu@proton.me>
Signed-off-by: Philippe Simons <simons.philippe@gmail.com>
---
 drivers/clk/qcom/clk-alpha-pll.c | 9 +++++++++
 drivers/clk/qcom/dispcc-sm8550.c | 2 +-
 2 files changed, 10 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/drivers/clk/qcom/clk-alpha-pll.c b/drivers/clk/qcom/clk-alpha-pll.c
--- a/drivers/clk/qcom/clk-alpha-pll.c
+++ b/drivers/clk/qcom/clk-alpha-pll.c
@@ -2372,6 +2372,15 @@ void clk_lucid_ole_pll_configure(struct clk_alpha_pll *pll, struct regmap *regma
 {
 	u32 lval = config->l;

+	/*
+	 * If the bootloader left the PLL enabled it's likely that there are
+	 * RCGs that will lock up if we disable the PLL below.
+	 */
+	if (trion_pll_is_enabled(pll, regmap)) {
+		pr_debug("Lucid Ole PLL is already enabled, skipping configuration\n");
+		return;
+	}
+
 	lval |= TRION_PLL_CAL_VAL << LUCID_EVO_PLL_CAL_L_VAL_SHIFT;
 	lval |= TRION_PLL_CAL_VAL << LUCID_OLE_PLL_RINGOSC_CAL_L_VAL_SHIFT;
 	clk_alpha_pll_write_config(regmap, PLL_L_VAL(pll), lval);
diff --git a/drivers/clk/qcom/dispcc-sm8550.c b/drivers/clk/qcom/dispcc-sm8550.c
--- a/drivers/clk/qcom/dispcc-sm8550.c
+++ b/drivers/clk/qcom/dispcc-sm8550.c
@@ -622,7 +622,7 @@ static struct clk_rcg2 disp_cc_mdss_mdp_clk_src = {
 		.parent_data = disp_cc_parent_data_8,
 		.num_parents = ARRAY_SIZE(disp_cc_parent_data_8),
 		.flags = CLK_SET_RATE_PARENT,
-		.ops = &clk_rcg2_shared_ops,
+		.ops = &clk_rcg2_shared_no_init_park_ops,
 	},
 };
